  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday night through next Saturday)\nIssued at 1148 PM CDT Fri Oct 30 2026\n\nTemperatures will be warmer Sunday as a shortwave upper ridge \nmoves east over Texas. May see some breaks in the clouds in the \nafternoon, but skies overall are expected to stay mostly cloudy \nwith moist southerly low-level flow. Expect highs to be the upper \n70s to lower 80s in our northern and central counties, and mid to \nupper 70s farther south. \n\nA large scale upper low and trough will move east into the \nwestern CONUS Sunday, and overspread the Rockies on Monday. A low \nis progged to develop in the southern portion of this trough over \nnorthern New Mexico, and then track across the Texas Panhandle and\nlift to the Kansas/Oklahoma border Monday night. Cold front \nassociated with this system is forecast to move southeast across \nour area Monday night. The track of the upper low and trough will \nbe important for rain chances late Monday and Monday night. A \ntrack a little farther to the north would, for the most part, \nresult in dry slotting for our area. At this time, appears that \nthere will be enough lift, moisture and instability for a band of \nshowers and thunderstorms to develop along the cold front, as it \npushes across our area. The higher rain chances and better \ncoverage of convection look to be in our eastern and southeastern \ncounties where more moisture should be available. Should also \nmention that SPC has the northern Big Country in a Day 4 Slight \nRisk of severe storms primarily for late Monday afternoon and \nevening. \n\nCooler air will follow this frontal passage and will be \naccompanied by breezy north-northwest winds on Tuesday. After a \nwarm day Monday with highs in the upper 70s to mid 80s, highs \nTuesday will be in the lower to mid 70s. May see some wrap around \ncloud cover over the northeast part of our area on Tuesday, as \nmoisture wraps around the departing upper system. Dry weather to \nfollow for Wednesday and Thursday, as surface high pressure \nsettles into Texas by Wednesday and shifts to the east on \nThursday. Dry weather to continue Friday with south winds \nreturning. After the period of well-above normal morning low \ntemperatures our area has seen in the past several days, will have\na couple of cool nights Tuesday night and Wednesday night with \nlows in the 40s. Daytime temperatures look pleasant Wednesday into\nFriday with forecast highs in the 70s.",
